WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.653
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:16.203
Unit fifteen: Statistical Reasoning. Today you will distinguish correlation from causation using precise clauses.

4
00:00:16.503 --> 00:00:19.353
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.753
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:25.676
Correlation.

7
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:30.575
Correlation means a statistical association between two variables.

8
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:34.576
A positive correlation means both variables rise together.

9
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:38.254
Your turn. Say: Correlation.

10
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.229
Correlation.

11
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:44.101
Causation.

12
00:00:45.117 --> 00:00:49.554
Causation means a relationship in which one factor directly produces another.

13
00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:53.264
Establishing causation usually requires an experiment.

14
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.851
Your turn. Say: Causation.

15
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:00.678
Causation.

16
00:01:01.417 --> 00:01:02.847
Sample size.

17
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.266
Sample size means the number of observations in a study.

18
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.929
A larger sample size increases statistical power.

19
00:01:11.517 --> 00:01:14.482
Your turn. Say: Sample size.

20
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:18.752
Sample size.

21
00:01:19.517 --> 00:01:20.750
Significance.

22
00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.549
Significance means the likelihood that a result is not due to chance.

23
00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:29.787
Statistical significance is not the same as practical importance.

24
00:01:30.383 --> 00:01:33.291
Your turn. Say: Significance.

25
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:37.413
Significance.

26
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:39.105
Variable.

27
00:01:40.117 --> 00:01:42.906
Variable means a measurable factor that can vary.

28
00:01:43.517 --> 00:01:46.062
Family income was a confounding variable.

29
00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.395
Your turn. Say: Variable.

30
00:01:52.233 --> 00:01:53.339
Variable.

31
00:01:54.083 --> 00:01:55.061
Outlier.

32
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:59.559
Outlier means a data point that differs markedly from the rest.

33
00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.568
One outlier dramatically inflated the average.

34
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:05.487
Your turn. Say: Outlier.

35
00:02:08.333 --> 00:02:09.427
Outlier.

36
00:02:10.183 --> 00:02:11.370
Distribution.

37
00:02:12.383 --> 00:02:16.264
Distribution means the pattern of how values are spread across a range.

38
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.418
Income has a highly skewed distribution.

39
00:02:20.017 --> 00:02:22.866
Your turn. Say: Distribution.

40
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:26.794
Distribution.

41
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:28.470
Bias.

42
00:02:29.483 --> 00:02:32.667
Bias means a systematic error that distorts results.

43
00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:36.096
Online polls suffer from self-selection bias.

44
00:02:36.683 --> 00:02:38.976
Your turn. Say: Bias.

45
00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:42.997
Bias.

46
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.404
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:55.059
Visal, a news site claims students who eat breakfast score higher, so breakfast boosts grades. Convinced?

48
00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:01.972
Not yet. Just because two variables correlate does not mean one causes the other.

49
00:03:03.083 --> 00:03:04.897
What else could explain the pattern?

50
00:03:05.983 --> 00:03:15.253
A confounding variable, such as family income. Wealthier families may provide both regular breakfasts and extra tutoring.

51
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.756
How could researchers establish causation?

52
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:27.843
Through a randomised experiment with a large sample size. Only then can other explanations be ruled out.

53
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.547
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:31.383 --> 00:03:35.763
Grammar focus: Distinguishing correlation vs causation clauses.

55
00:03:36.063 --> 00:03:37.261
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:41.865
Just because two variables correlate does not mean one causes the other.

57
00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:46.456
The fact that the result is significant does not prove that it is important.

58
00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:51.602
Just because sales rose after the advert does not mean the advert caused the rise.

59
00:03:52.567 --> 00:04:01.384
'Just because X does not mean Y' is common in speech; 'The fact that X does not prove that Y' is more formal. Both warn against jumping from association to cause.

60
00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:06.555
Be careful! Don't say: Just because they correlate doesn't means causation.

61
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.556
Say: Just because they correlate doesn't mean causation.

62
00:04:11.856 --> 00:04:13.856
After 'doesn't', use the base verb.

63
00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:17.296
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.542
When Numbers Mislead.

65
00:04:19.842 --> 00:04:23.676
Statistics are powerful tools, but they are also easily misinterpreted.

66
00:04:24.667 --> 00:04:28.431
Just because two variables correlate does not mean one causes the other.

67
00:04:29.433 --> 00:04:35.345
A hidden confounding variable may drive both, creating an association that is entirely spurious.

68
00:04:36.333 --> 00:04:43.244
Likewise, a small sample size or a single extreme outlier can produce results that appear significant but fail to replicate.

69
00:04:44.233 --> 00:04:49.298
Rigorous researchers therefore treat every correlation as a question rather than an answer.

70
00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.007
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:58.707
Just because two variables correlate does not blank one causes the other.

72
00:04:59.007 --> 00:05:02.115
A, mean. B, means. C, meaning.

73
00:05:05.667 --> 00:05:07.539
The answer is A: mean.

74
00:05:08.683 --> 00:05:13.507
Breakfast and grades are both linked to family income. Income is a blank variable.

75
00:05:13.807 --> 00:05:17.541
A, confounding. B, dependent. C, significant.

76
00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.274
The answer is A: confounding.

77
00:05:24.417 --> 00:05:27.078
Which method is best for establishing causation?

78
00:05:27.378 --> 00:05:32.471
A, a randomised experiment. B, an online poll. C, a single case.

79
00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:38.846
The answer is A: a randomised experiment.

80
00:05:39.983 --> 00:05:43.852
The fact that a result is significant does not prove blank it is important.

81
00:05:44.152 --> 00:05:47.237
A, what. B, that. C, which.

82
00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:52.440
The answer is B: that.

83
00:05:53.583 --> 00:05:57.661
According to the reading, how should researchers treat a correlation?

84
00:05:57.961 --> 00:06:02.218
A, as proof. B, as a question. C, as an outlier.

85
00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:08.138
The answer is B: as a question.

86
00:06:09.333 --> 00:06:12.566
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:13.417 --> 00:06:17.076
Can you give an example of correlation being mistaken for causation?

88
00:06:33.883 --> 00:06:35.488
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:35.788 --> 00:07:06.637
A well-known example is the correlation between ice cream sales and drowning incidents. Both rise during the summer, but just because they correlate does not mean ice cream causes drowning. The real explanation is a third variable: hot weather, which leads to more swimming and more ice cream. People make this mistake because a clear pattern feels like an explanation. That is why researchers rely on controlled experiments before claiming causation.

90
00:07:06.937 --> 00:07:08.612
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:09.517 --> 00:07:15.350
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and distinguishing correlation vs causation clauses.

92
00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:20.137
Today's value is analytical rigor. Test the numbers before you believe the story.

93
00:07:20.437 --> 00:07:23.240
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.809
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

